#%PAM-1.0
# LionOS greeter AUTOMATIC-LOGIN stack.
#
# This stack is entered ONLY by lion-greeter's autologin path, and only
# for the single user named in /etc/lionos/greeter.toml [autologin].
# The daemon enforces that 1:1 mapping itself (it resolves the user and
# runs this stack for exactly that account), so pam_permit here cannot
# be abused as a general "anyone may log in" hole through any other
# caller: no other program is configured to use this service name.
#
# Site policy belongs IN THIS FILE, not in the greeter:
#   * to restrict autologin to a group, replace pam_permit with:
#       auth  required  pam_succeed_if.so user ingroup lion-autologin
#   * to require a plugged security key even for autologin, add the
#     pam_u2f / pam_fido2 auth line before pam_permit and change
#     pam_permit to "sufficient".
#   * to disable autologin entirely, delete [autologin] user from
#     greeter.toml — or make the first line `auth requisite pam_deny.so`.
#
# Phase mapping mirrors pam.d/lion-greeter so the session environment
# (logind registration, loginuid, keyrings, limits) is IDENTICAL whether
# the user typed a password or not. pam_nologin still hard-fails if
# /etc/nologin exists: autologin must never bypass an admin lockdown.
auth      requisite  pam_nologin.so
auth      sufficient pam_permit.so
account   required   pam_nologin.so
@include common-account
session   required   pam_loginuid.so
@include common-session
session   optional   pam_keyinit.so force revoke
